#!/usr/bin/env bash
# mesh-tg-inbound — the INBOUND-LIVENESS sense for the operator's Telegram channel.
#
# WHY (incident 2026-08-18, priority:incident): the mesh was TG-DEAF for 20.5h and NOTHING noticed,
# because the only sign of "we are receiving" was `systemctl --user is-active mesh-voice-rx` reading
# `active/running`. ACTIVE IS NOT RECEIVING. mesh-voice-rx's poll loop prints nothing on a successful
# getUpdates (scripts/mesh-voice-rx:1020 poll_once — only the FAILURE branch logs), and it never dies
# on a 409, it backs off and keeps looping. So a second poller on the same token puts the unit in a
# permanent, silent, green-looking wedge: Telegram answers exactly ONE getUpdates and 409s the other,
# every operator message lands in whichever node won that race, and the losing node shows no missing
# line, no failed unit, no stale mtime on anything a watchdog reads. Measured on this node while
# writing this tool: 19241 `Conflict: terminated by other getUpdates request` lines in 26h, still
# firing, unit `active/running` the whole time. The OPERATOR noticed before any reflex did.
#
# mesh-guardian already carries the CURE for that shape (the primary/standby leg, 2026-08-18) — this
# is the missing SENSE. A cure with no sense heals a fault nobody can see start or stop.
#
# THE ARTIFACT is the AGE of the last inbound line in ~/.mesh/voice-in.log carried TOGETHER WITH the
# unit state — neither alone is a reading. Age alone cannot tell a quiet operator from a deaf mesh;
# unit state alone is the very lie that cost 20.5h.
#
# THE DISCRIMINATOR — 'входящих не было' vs 'мы не слышим' must be different strings, and this tool
# earns the difference with a real artifact instead of a guess: `getWebhookInfo` returns
# `pending_update_count`, the count of updates Telegram is HOLDING for this token. It neither consumes
# updates nor competes with getUpdates (verified live: probe returned ok while our poller was mid-409),
# so it is safe to call from a cron sense. queue=0 is POSITIVE evidence that nothing is waiting →
# the silence is the operator's. queue>0 and undrained → messages exist and nobody is taking them →
# we are deaf, proven. queue=na (no token / probe failed) is NEITHER — it renders `na`, never 0, and
# a stale inbound under a blind probe is RED-as-UNPROVEN, never green-as-quiet.
# The queue probe alone is NOT sufficient and never replaces the conflict leg: when a COMPETING poller
# drains the token, the queue reads 0 while WE hear nothing. That is exactly this node's live state
# (queue=0, conflict streak firing) and it is why both legs exist.
#
# THE HOST GATE — a verdict about an ear this node does not have is not a reading (2026-08-27).
# Measured: `[fyi] tg-inbound NOEAR on phaedra: ... the mesh is not listening to Telegram at all` was
# posted to the board 112 times between 2026-08-22T18:30:08Z and 2026-08-27T16:30:09Z, every one of
# them from a node whose own line reads `load=not-found,proc=0` — phaedra has never hosted the ear.
# mesh-home held it the entire time (HEARING, queue=0), so the sentence was false at every firing, and
# it was a GLOBAL sentence: "the mesh is not listening", from a node that could not know.
# THE TOKEN IS NOT THE PREDICATE, and this is the trap worth stating because the obvious gate looks
# right. The credential file is REPLICATED, not owned: phaedra's own posts carry `queue=1`, and a
# number in that field is only reachable when read_queue found a BOT_TOKEN — so a "token present OR
# unit declared" gate would have suppressed ZERO of the 112. Holding the credential is not hosting the
# organ. The predicate is the ORGAN: systemd declares the unit here, or a poller process is alive here
# (mesh-guardian's setsid takeover has no unit, and it IS an ear).
# AND THE GATE MAY ONLY SPEND POSITIVE EVIDENCE. `not-found` is systemd's own ANSWER; `absent` is this
# tool's word for "we could not ask" (the cron/XDG blindness documented below, which reached leg 1 for
# weeks). Only the answer buys silence — a blind read falls through and still alarms, because a sense
# that goes n/a whenever it cannot see is the fabricated-calm shape one ring out.
# MESH_TG_IN_REQUIRE_EAR=1 forces the ear to be expected on a node that declares it must host.
#
# VERDICTS (precedence order; each is its own string — nothing collapses into "quiet"):
#   NOHOST   systemd ANSWERS not-found for the unit and no poller runs here — this node does not
#            host the ear, so every leg below would be about another node's.            n/a (rc 2)
#   NOEAR    unit not active AND no poller process, on a node that DOES host the ear —
#            THIS NODE is not listening. Node-local; it makes no claim about the mesh.   RED
#   CONFLICT trailing consecutive `Conflict: terminated by other getUpdates request` streak
#            >= M with its last line inside the window — a second poller holds the token.  RED
#   BACKLOG  queue >= K — Telegram is holding updates and nobody drains them.              RED
#   UNPROVEN unit active, no conflict, last inbound older than N, queue BLIND — we cannot
#            tell a quiet operator from a deaf mesh, and unproven is not green.            RED
#   QUIET    unit active, no conflict, queue==0 (probe LIVE), last inbound older than N —
#            nothing is waiting for us; the silence is the operator's, not our deafness.   ok
#   NODATA   voice-in.log absent/empty/unparseable — we have never had an inbound line to
#            age. NOT rendered as silence, NOT rendered as 0.                              see below
#   HEARING  unit active, no conflict, last inbound within N.                              ok
#
#   NODATA is RED when the queue probe is blind or the queue is non-empty, and `na` (exit 2) on a
#   fresh install where the unit is up and the queue is provably empty — a node that has simply never
#   been messaged is not a fault, and inventing an age of 0 for it would be the fabricated-calm shape.
#
#   mesh-tg-inbound            one pass: read, write state, append log, board-post a RED (debounced)
#   mesh-tg-inbound --check    read-only: same reading, no state write, no board post
#   mesh-tg-inbound --status   last state line, no new read
#   mesh-tg-inbound --test     drives every verdict from fixtures, VISIBLE-RED gate on the two
#                              incident conditions, + a REAL read of all four legs on this node
#
# exit 0 = ok (HEARING/QUIET) · 1 = RED · 2 = honest n/a (NOHOST: no ear on this node / never messaged)
#
# reflex-cadence: */10 * * * *
# reflex-state: $HOME/.mesh/.tg-inbound.state
# reflex-verdict-field: 2
# ^ this state line leads with a TIMESTAMP, so mesh-reflex-health's value-frozen axis could watch
#   neither field 1 (a clock is not a verdict — it refuses to guess) nor the whole content (the same
#   clock moves every run, so the frozen clock reset every run and the check was permanently green).
#   This reflex was unmonitored for freeze on BOTH axes until 2026-08-29. Field 2 is the verdict.
#   Keep this in step with the state line below: move the verdict column and this must move with it.
set -uo pipefail
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"   # cron self-defense (siblings by bare name)

UNIT="${MESH_TG_IN_UNIT:-mesh-voice-rx.service}"
VLOG="${MESH_TG_IN_LOG:-$HOME/.mesh/voice-in.log}"
STATE="${MESH_TG_IN_STATE:-$HOME/.mesh/.tg-inbound.state}"
OLOG="${MESH_TG_IN_LOGFILE:-$HOME/.mesh/tg-inbound.log}"
MARK="${MESH_TG_IN_MARK:-$HOME/.mesh/.tg-inbound.posted}"
CRED="${MESH_TG_IN_CRED:-$HOME/.config/remote-access/env}"
MAX_AGE_MIN="${MESH_TG_IN_MAX_AGE_MIN:-240}"      # N — inbound older than this is stale
CONFLICT_M="${MESH_TG_IN_CONFLICT_STREAK:-3}"     # M — consecutive Conflict lines that mean a rival poller
WINDOW_MIN="${MESH_TG_IN_WINDOW_MIN:-15}"         # the streak's last line must be this recent
QUEUE_K="${MESH_TG_IN_QUEUE_RED:-1}"              # queue >= K is a backlog
DEBOUNCE_MIN="${MESH_TG_IN_DEBOUNCE_MIN:-60}"     # one board post per verdict per this many minutes
CHAT="${MESH_TG_IN_CHAT:-1}"
CONFLICT_RE='Conflict: terminated by other getUpdates request'   # the EXACT Telegram string, not a guess
NODE="${MESH_TG_IN_NODE:-$(hostname)}"            # every verdict is NODE-LOCAL and must say whose ear it is about

now_epoch(){ date -u +%s; }
stamp(){ date -u +%FT%TZ; }

# --- cron self-defense, second half: THE USER BUS -------------------------------------------------
# `systemctl --user` and `journalctl --user` need XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS, and
# cron hands a job neither. Under this tool's OWN */10 cadence every systemd read therefore failed
# with "Failed to connect to bus: No medium found": LoadState came back empty, so leg 1 rendered
# `load=absent` (falling through to the process probe) and leg 3 took its `[ "$load" = loaded ] ||
# return na` arm and rendered `conflict=na` — permanently, on every tick since it was wired.
# Measured 2026-08-21: the sensor that exists to detect a RIVAL POLLER wrote conflict=na straight
# through the 01:25->02:51Z 409 storm, while the truth sat in the very journal it could not open, and
# the only reason we found the second poller at all was a human reading journalctl by hand.
# THE TRAP IS NOT THE na — the na was honest, and leg 3's comment above is right that na and 0 must
# differ. The trap is that na was the ONLY REACHABLE STATE: a distinction between "no conflict" and
# "could not look" buys nothing when the second is unconditional. Sibling of heavy-run-cron-xdg.
# Derive both from the uid; where there is genuinely no bus, leave them unset so the na stays
# truthful instead of becoming a lie in the other direction.
_ensure_user_bus(){
  local uid rt
  uid="$(id -u)"
  rt="${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$uid}"
  [ -d "$rt" ] || return 0
  export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR="$rt"
  [ -n "${DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS:-}" ] && return 0
  [ -S "$rt/bus" ] || return 0
  # MEASURED, not assumed: on this node XDG_RUNTIME_DIR alone is the load-bearing half — a mutant
  # that never sets DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS still passes gate (14), because systemd derives the bus
  # path from the runtime dir. The export is kept for environments where the socket is elsewhere, and
  # is recorded here as a SURVIVING mutant rather than silently trusted: the gate proves the union of
  # the two, never either one on its own.
  export DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS="unix:path=$rt/bus"
  return 0
}
_ensure_user_bus

# --- leg 1: the unit ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reads systemd first, falls back to a process probe, because mesh-guardian's takeover path starts the
# poller with setsid (tg_start) and NOT as a unit — a systemd-only read would call that running poller
# absent. Renders which source answered, so a reader is never guessing.
# stdin = `ps -eo comm,args`. Counts ONLY the poller: comm is the python interpreter AND the argv
# mentions mesh-voice-rx. A bare `grep -c mesh-voice-rx` over argv counts the READER's own children —
# this tool's `journalctl --user -u mesh-voice-rx.service` is a live process whose cmdline contains the
# string, so the naive probe reported proc=1 with the unit STOPPED and the whole sense read HEARING
# while the mesh was deaf. Caught only by driving the gate red for real (2026-08-18); a fixture-only
# test would have passed it. Same family as pkill-f-kills-its-own-wrapper-shell.
_count_poller(){ awk '$1 ~ /^python3?/ && /mesh-voice-rx/' | wc -l; }

read_unit(){
  if [ -n "${MESH_TG_IN_UNIT_CMD:-}" ]; then eval "$MESH_TG_IN_UNIT_CMD"; return; fi
  local load act sub n
  load="$(systemctl --user show "$UNIT" -p LoadState --value 2>/dev/null)"
  act="$(systemctl --user show "$UNIT" -p ActiveState --value 2>/dev/null)"
  sub="$(systemctl --user show "$UNIT" -p SubState --value 2>/dev/null)"
  n="$(ps -eo comm,args 2>/dev/null | _count_poller)"
  [ -n "$load" ] || load=absent
  printf '%s|%s|%s|%s\n' "$load" "${act:-unknown}" "${sub:-unknown}" "${n:-0}"
}

# --- leg 2: the age of the last INBOUND line ------------------------------------------------------
# The line format is `<ISO8601Z>  <KIND>  <text>` (mesh-voice-rx write()). An unparseable/absent/empty
# log yields `nodata`, never an age of 0 — a fabricated 0 would read as "an inbound arrived just now",
# the exact inverse of the truth.
read_lastin(){
  # Resolved HERE, not at load: a load-time global cannot be re-pointed by an env prefix on an
  # in-process call, so the test would silently read the node's real log while believing it drove a
  # fixture — a fixture that never reached the code under test.
  local VLOG="${MESH_TG_IN_LOG:-$HOME/.mesh/voice-in.log}"
  local ts age
  [ -s "$VLOG" ] || { echo "nodata|nodata"; return; }
  ts="$(grep -oE '^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}T[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}Z' "$VLOG" 2>/dev/null | tail -1)"
  [ -n "$ts" ] || { echo "nodata|nodata"; return; }
  age="$(date -u -d "$ts" +%s 2>/dev/null)" || { echo "nodata|nodata"; return; }
  [ -n "$age" ] || { echo "nodata|nodata"; return; }
  printf '%s|%s\n' "$ts" "$(( ( $(now_epoch) - age ) / 60 ))"
}

# --- leg 3: the conflict streak -------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONSECUTIVE trailing lines, not a count: a chronic rival poller writes thousands of these, but so
# would a single bad minute during a restart — what separates them is that the tail is UNBROKEN and
# CURRENT. A blind journal (no unit loaded / journalctl refused) renders `na`, never 0: "we saw no
# conflict" and "we could not look" are the difference between a green reflex and an honest one.
read_conflict(){
  if [ -n "${MESH_TG_IN_JOURNAL_CMD:-}" ]; then
    local out; out="$(eval "$MESH_TG_IN_JOURNAL_CMD" 2>/dev/null)" || { echo "na|na"; return; }
    printf '%s\n' "$out" | _streak; return
  fi
  local load; load="$(systemctl --user show "$UNIT" -p LoadState --value 2>/dev/null)"
  [ "$load" = loaded ] || { echo "na|na"; return; }
  local out
  out="$(journalctl --user -u "$UNIT" --since "-${WINDOW_MIN}min" --no-pager -q -o short-iso 2>/dev/null)" \
    || { echo "na|na"; return; }
  printf '%s\n' "$out" | _streak
}
# stdin = the unit's journal lines for the window, oldest first. Emits `<streak>|<total>`.
_streak(){
  awk -v re="$CONFLICT_RE" '
    NF==0 { next }
    { total++; if (index($0, re)) streak++; else streak=0 }
    END { printf "%d|%d\n", streak+0, total+0 }'
}

# --- leg 4: the queue (the discriminator) ---------------------------------------------------------
# getWebhookInfo.pending_update_count — what Telegram is HOLDING for this token. Read-only w.r.t. the
# update queue: it does not consume, does not advance the offset, does not 409 the live poller.
read_queue(){
  if [ -n "${MESH_TG_IN_PROBE_CMD:-}" ]; then eval "$MESH_TG_IN_PROBE_CMD"; return; fi
  local CRED="${MESH_TG_IN_CRED:-$HOME/.config/remote-access/env}"
  local tok body n
  tok="$(grep -s '^[[:space:]]*BOT_TOKEN=' "$CRED" 2>/dev/null | head -1 | cut -d= -f2- | tr -d '"'"'"' \r')"
  [ -n "$tok" ] || [ -n "${MESH_TG_IN_PROBE_BODY+x}" ] || { echo na; return; }
  if [ -n "${MESH_TG_IN_PROBE_BODY+x}" ]; then body="$MESH_TG_IN_PROBE_BODY"
  else body="$(curl -s --max-time 15 "https://api.telegram.org/bot${tok}/getWebhookInfo" 2>/dev/null)" || { echo na; return; }; fi
  case "$body" in *'"ok":true'*) : ;; *) echo na; return;; esac
  n="$(printf '%s' "$body" | grep -oE '"pending_update_count":[0-9]+' | grep -oE '[0-9]+$')"
  [ -n "$n" ] || { echo na; return; }
  printf '%s\n' "$n"
}

# --- the verdict ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pure: takes the four legs, returns `<verdict>|<rc>|<sentence>`. Kept separate from the reads so the
# test can drive every branch without a bot token, a journal, or a poller.
decide(){
  local uload="$1" uact="$2" usub="$3" uproc="$4" lastage="$5" streak="$6" queue="$7"
  local running=0
  { [ "$uact" = active ] && [ "$usub" = running ]; } && running=1
  [ "${uproc:-0}" -ge 1 ] 2>/dev/null && running=1
  # THE HOST GATE (see the header). `not-found` is systemd's ANSWER and buys silence; `absent`/empty is
  # our own blindness and buys nothing — it falls through to NOEAR, which is now node-scoped anyway.
  if [ "$running" = 0 ] && [ "$uload" = not-found ] && [ "${MESH_TG_IN_REQUIRE_EAR:-0}" != 1 ]; then
    printf 'NOHOST|2|%s does not host the TG ear: systemd ANSWERS LoadState=not-found for %s and no poller process is alive here, so the inbound age, the queue and the conflict streak below would all be about the ear on ANOTHER node. No reading to give here (n/a, not a fault, and NOT a claim about the mesh)\n' \
      "$NODE" "$UNIT"; return
  fi
  if [ "$running" = 0 ]; then
    printf 'NOEAR|1|no poller ON %s: unit %s is %s/%s (load=%s) and no mesh-voice-rx process is alive — THIS NODE is not listening to Telegram. Node-local verdict: it says NOTHING about whether another node holds the ear\n' \
      "$NODE" "$UNIT" "$uact" "$usub" "$uload"; return
  fi
  if [ "$streak" != na ] && [ "$streak" -ge "$CONFLICT_M" ] 2>/dev/null; then
    printf 'CONFLICT|1|a SECOND poller holds this bot token: %s consecutive "%s" in the last %smin — Telegram answers one getUpdates and 409s ours, so operator messages are landing on the other node and are INVISIBLE here (no missing line, no failed unit)\n' \
      "$streak" "$CONFLICT_RE" "$WINDOW_MIN"; return
  fi
  if [ "$queue" != na ] && [ "$queue" -ge "$QUEUE_K" ] 2>/dev/null; then
    printf 'BACKLOG|1|Telegram is holding %s update(s) for this token and nobody is draining them — the poller is up but not receiving\n' "$queue"; return
  fi
  if [ "$lastage" = nodata ]; then
    if [ "$queue" = na ]; then
      printf 'NODATA|1|no inbound line has EVER been parsed from %s and the queue probe is blind — this is "we have no evidence we can hear", NOT "the operator is silent"\n' "$VLOG"; return
    fi
    printf 'NODATA|2|no inbound line has ever been parsed from %s; queue is provably empty (%s) so nothing is being lost — never messaged here, not a fault (n/a, not 0)\n' "$VLOG" "$queue"; return
  fi
  if [ "$lastage" -gt "$MAX_AGE_MIN" ] 2>/dev/null; then
    if [ "$queue" = na ]; then
      printf 'UNPROVEN|1|last inbound was %smin ago (> %smin) and the queue probe is BLIND — we cannot tell a quiet operator from a deaf mesh, and unproven is not green\n' "$lastage" "$MAX_AGE_MIN"; return
    fi
    printf 'QUIET|0|no inbound for %smin (> %smin), and Telegram is holding %s update(s) — nothing is waiting for us, so this is the OPERATOR being silent, not the mesh being deaf\n' "$lastage" "$MAX_AGE_MIN" "$queue"; return
  fi
  printf 'HEARING|0|last inbound %smin ago (<= %smin), no conflict streak, queue=%s\n' "$lastage" "$MAX_AGE_MIN" "$queue"
}

# --- one pass -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
pass(){   # $1 = write? (1 = advance state/log/board, 0 = --check read-only)
  local write="$1" u ul ua us up li lts lage cf st tot q v rc msg line
  u="$(read_unit)";     ul="${u%%|*}"; u="${u#*|}"; ua="${u%%|*}"; u="${u#*|}"; us="${u%%|*}"; up="${u##*|}"
  li="$(read_lastin)";  lts="${li%%|*}"; lage="${li##*|}"
  cf="$(read_conflict)"; st="${cf%%|*}"; tot="${cf##*|}"
  q="$(read_queue)"
  v="$(decide "$ul" "$ua" "$us" "$up" "$lage" "$st" "$q")"
  rc="${v#*|}"; rc="${rc%%|*}"; msg="${v#*|*|}"; v="${v%%|*}"
  # Every field is NAMED and every unknown says `na`. `last-in=nodata` and `last-in=<n>m` are
  # different strings on purpose; so are `conflict=na` (could not look) and `conflict=0` (looked, none).
  line="$(stamp) $v unit=$ua/$us(load=$ul,proc=$up) last-in=$( [ "$lage" = nodata ] && echo nodata || echo "${lage}m" ) last-in-ts=$lts conflict=$st/${tot}ln/${WINDOW_MIN}min queue=$q thresholds=N${MAX_AGE_MIN}m/M${CONFLICT_M}/K${QUEUE_K} :: $msg"
  printf '%s\n' "$line"
  [ "$write" = 1 ] || return "$rc"
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$STATE")" 2>/dev/null
  printf '%s\n' "$line" > "$STATE"
  printf '%s\n' "$line" >> "$OLOG"
  # mtime = ran-live, content = the reading (liveness-touch convention: a value that holds for days
  # must not read as a dead reflex to mesh-reflex-health/mesh-pulse).
  command -v mesh-state-touch >/dev/null 2>&1 && mesh-state-touch "$STATE" >/dev/null 2>&1
  if [ "$rc" = 1 ] && [ "$CHAT" = 1 ] && command -v mesh-chat >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    local last=0 nowe; nowe="$(now_epoch)"
    [ -f "$MARK" ] && last="$(awk -v v="$v" '$1==v{print $2}' "$MARK" 2>/dev/null | tail -1)"
    [ -n "$last" ] || last=0
    if [ "$(( nowe - last ))" -ge "$(( DEBOUNCE_MIN * 60 ))" ]; then
      MESH_WHO="tg-inbound@$(hostname)" mesh-chat "[fyi] tg-inbound $v on $(hostname): $msg | $line" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
      { grep -v "^$v " "$MARK" 2>/dev/null; printf '%s %s\n' "$v" "$nowe"; } > "$MARK.tmp" && mv "$MARK.tmp" "$MARK"
    fi
  fi
  return "$rc"
}

# --- test -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
run_test(){
  local fails=0 out
  F(){ echo "smoke-test: FAIL ($1)" >&2; fails=$((fails+1)); }
  # A gate you have not seen FAIL is not a gate: every verdict below is driven from injected legs, so
  # the branch is exercised even on a node with no token, no journal and no poller.
  #
  # (1) VISIBLE RED #1 — the incident itself: unit active/running, a live conflict streak. This is the
  #     state this node was in for 26h while every existing check read green.
  out="$(decide loaded active running 1 5 9 0)"
  case "$out" in CONFLICT\|1\|*) : ;; *) F "active+conflict-streak did not go RED CONFLICT (got: ${out%%|*})";; esac
  # ...and the same conflict count BELOW M must not fire — a threshold that fires at every value is not
  # a threshold.
  out="$(decide loaded active running 1 5 2 0)"
  case "$out" in CONFLICT*) F "conflict streak below M (2 < 3) fired anyway";; esac
  # (2) VISIBLE RED #2 — reception stopped: unit active, no conflict, inbound stale, probe blind.
  out="$(decide loaded active running 1 1300 0 na)"
  case "$out" in UNPROVEN\|1\|*) : ;; *) F "active+stale+blind-probe did not go RED UNPROVEN (got: ${out%%|*})";; esac
  # (3) THE DISCRIMINATOR — the identical staleness with a LIVE probe reading an empty queue is the
  #     operator being quiet, and it must be a DIFFERENT string with a DIFFERENT rc. If these two ever
  #     collapse into one verdict, the tool is back to rendering silence as deafness.
  out="$(decide loaded active running 1 1300 0 0)"
  case "$out" in QUIET\|0\|*) : ;; *) F "stale+empty-queue did not render QUIET (got: ${out%%|*})";; esac
  [ "$(decide loaded active running 1 1300 0 na | cut -d'|' -f1)" \
    != "$(decide loaded active running 1 1300 0 0  | cut -d'|' -f1)" ] \
    || F "'we cannot hear' and 'the operator was silent' render the SAME verdict string"
  # (4) queue backlog: updates exist, nobody drains them — deaf, proven, even with zero conflict.
  out="$(decide loaded active running 1 5 0 7)"
  case "$out" in BACKLOG\|1\|*) : ;; *) F "undrained queue did not go RED BACKLOG (got: ${out%%|*})";; esac
  # (5) no poller at all — a different sentence from every flavour of "up but not hearing".
  out="$(decide loaded inactive dead 0 5 na na)"
  case "$out" in NOEAR\|1\|*) : ;; *) F "dead unit + no process did not go RED NOEAR (got: ${out%%|*})";; esac
  # ...but a setsid-started poller with NO unit is still an ear (mesh-guardian's takeover path).
  out="$(decide absent unknown unknown 1 5 na 0)"
  case "$out" in NOEAR*) F "a running non-unit poller was called NOEAR";; esac
  # (5a) THE HOST GATE — phaedra's EXACT live reading, replayed field for field: systemd answered
  #      not-found, no poller, an inbound 9402min old, and queue=1 (which proves the token IS present
  #      there — the token can never be the predicate). It must be NOHOST/rc2, and it must be neither
  #      of the two alarms it used to fire: not NOEAR (112 board posts over 5 days) and not BACKLOG.
  out="$(decide not-found inactive dead 0 9402 na 1)"
  case "$out" in NOHOST\|2\|*) : ;; *) F "a node systemd says has no unit and no poller did not render NOHOST rc2 (got: ${out%%|*})";; esac
  case "$out" in NOEAR*|BACKLOG*) F "the non-host node still fired a verdict about an ear it does not have (${out%%|*})";; esac
  # ...and the NOHOST sentence must SAY it is n/a rather than healthy — a silent 'ok' here is worse
  # than the false alarm it replaces.
  case "$out" in *"does not host"*) : ;; *) F "the NOHOST sentence does not say this node has no ear";; esac
  # (5b) BLINDNESS MUST NOT BUY SILENCE. `absent` is this tool's OWN word for a systemd read that
  #      never happened (the cron/XDG failure documented at the top, which held leg 1 for weeks);
  #      `not-found` is systemd's answer. Only the answer may gate. If these collapse, the sense goes
  #      quiet on exactly the ticks it could not see.
  out="$(decide absent inactive dead 0 5 na na)"
  case "$out" in NOHOST*) F "a BLIND unit read (load=absent) was treated as proof this node has no ear — the gate goes silent whenever it cannot look";; esac
  case "$out" in NOEAR\|1\|*) : ;; *) F "a blind unit read with no poller did not still alarm (got: ${out%%|*})";; esac
  [ "$(decide not-found inactive dead 0 5 na 0 | cut -d'|' -f1)" \
    != "$(decide absent    inactive dead 0 5 na 0 | cut -d'|' -f1)" ] \
    || F "'systemd says there is no unit here' and 'we could not ask systemd' render the SAME verdict"
  # (5c) the guardian takeover path is an EAR even with no unit — it must not be gated away.
  out="$(decide not-found unknown unknown 1 5 na 0)"
  case "$out" in NOHOST*) F "a live setsid poller (mesh-guardian takeover, no unit) was gated away as NOHOST";; esac
  # (5d) a node that DECLARES it must host still alarms on the same reading — the gate is an honest
  #      n/a, never an off-switch for the incident it was built for.
  out="$(MESH_TG_IN_REQUIRE_EAR=1 decide not-found inactive dead 0 5 na 0)"
  case "$out" in NOEAR\|1\|*) : ;; *) F "MESH_TG_IN_REQUIRE_EAR=1 did not force the alarm back on (got: ${out%%|*})";; esac
  # (5e) THE SENTENCE IS NODE-LOCAL. The defect was not only WHO alarmed, it was WHAT the alarm said:
  #      a node with no ear posted "the mesh is not listening to Telegram at all" 112 times.
  out="$(decide loaded inactive dead 0 5 na 0)"
  case "$out" in *"the mesh is not listening"*) F "the NOEAR sentence still makes a GLOBAL claim about the mesh from one node's reading";; esac
  case "$out" in *"$NODE"*) : ;; *) F "the NOEAR sentence does not name the node it is about";; esac
  case "$out" in *"NOTHING about whether another node holds the ear"*) : ;; *) F "the NOEAR sentence does not disclaim the mesh-wide reading";; esac
  # (5f) END TO END, WITH A CONTROL ARM: NOHOST must reach no board post, and the ONLY way to know
  #      that is to prove the same harness DOES post on a RED. The stub goes in a FAKE HOME's
  #      .local/bin because the script exports "$HOME/.local/bin" to the FRONT of PATH — a stub dir
  #      merely prepended by the test loses that race to the deployed twin
  #      ([[a-stub-on-path-can-lose-a-path-race-to-the-real-one]]).
  local fh; fh="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$fh/.local/bin"
  printf '#!/bin/sh\nprintf "%%s\\n" "$*" >> "%s/posted"\n' "$fh" > "$fh/.local/bin/mesh-chat"
  chmod +x "$fh/.local/bin/mesh-chat"
  _e2e(){ env HOME="$fh" MESH_TG_IN_STATE="$fh/st" MESH_TG_IN_LOGFILE="$fh/lg" MESH_TG_IN_MARK="$fh/mk" \
      MESH_TG_IN_CHAT=1 MESH_TG_IN_UNIT_CMD="echo \"$1\"" MESH_TG_IN_JOURNAL_CMD='true' \
      MESH_TG_IN_PROBE_CMD='echo 0' MESH_TG_IN_LOG=/nonexistent-voice-in MESH_TG_IN_CRED=/nonexistent-cred \
      bash "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?; }
  out="$(_e2e 'loaded|inactive|dead|0')"
  [ "$out" = 1 ] || F "control arm: a host node with a dead ear did not exit 1 (got $out)"
  [ -s "$fh/posted" ] || F "control arm: the stubbed mesh-chat was never reached — the 'NOHOST does not post' assertion below would be vacuous"
  rm -f "$fh/posted" "$fh/st" "$fh/lg" "$fh/mk"
  out="$(_e2e 'not-found|inactive|dead|0')"
  [ "$out" = 2 ] || F "a non-host node did not exit 2 (got $out)"
  [ -e "$fh/posted" ] && F "NOHOST posted to the board — the 112-posts-in-5-days defect is not closed"
  grep -q NOHOST "$fh/st" 2>/dev/null || F "the NOHOST reading was not written to the state file (--status would say nothing)"
  rm -rf "$fh"
  # (6) NODATA is never 0 and never 'quiet': blind → RED, provably-empty queue → honest n/a (rc 2).
  out="$(decide loaded active running 1 nodata 0 na)"
  case "$out" in NODATA\|1\|*) : ;; *) F "nodata+blind-probe was not RED (got: ${out%%|*})";; esac
  out="$(decide loaded active running 1 nodata 0 0)"
  case "$out" in NODATA\|2\|*) : ;; *) F "nodata+empty-queue was not honest n/a rc2 (got: ${out%%|*})";; esac
  case "$out" in *"never messaged"*) : ;; *) F "the never-messaged sentence does not say so";; esac
  # (7) green
  out="$(decide loaded active running 1 3 0 0)"
  case "$out" in HEARING\|0\|*) : ;; *) F "healthy reading was not HEARING (got: ${out%%|*})";; esac
  # (8) the streak is CONSECUTIVE and CURRENT, not a count. A journal with conflicts followed by any
  #     later line has streak 0 — otherwise a restart's brief 409 burst reads as a permanent rival.
  out="$(printf 'a %s\nb %s\nc recovered\n' "$CONFLICT_RE" "$CONFLICT_RE" | _streak)"
  [ "$out" = "0|3" ] || F "a broken streak was not reset (got $out)"
  out="$(printf 'x quiet\ny %s\nz %s\n' "$CONFLICT_RE" "$CONFLICT_RE" | _streak)"
  [ "$out" = "2|3" ] || F "trailing streak miscounted (got $out)"
  # (9) an EMPTY journal window is 0 conflicts, and a REFUSED journal is na — collapsing these is how
  #     a blind sense reads green.
  out="$(MESH_TG_IN_JOURNAL_CMD='true' read_conflict)"
  [ "$out" = "0|0" ] || F "empty journal window did not read 0 (got $out)"
  out="$(MESH_TG_IN_JOURNAL_CMD='exit 1' read_conflict)"
  [ "$out" = "na|na" ] || F "a refused journal did not render na (got $out)"
  # (10) the queue probe renders na — never 0 — on every failure mode.
  out="$(MESH_TG_IN_PROBE_BODY='{"ok":false,"error_code":401}' read_queue)"; [ "$out" = na ] || F "probe ok:false did not render na (got $out)"
  out="$(MESH_TG_IN_PROBE_BODY='{"ok":true,"result":{"url":""}}' read_queue)"; [ "$out" = na ] || F "a body with no pending_update_count did not render na (got $out)"
  out="$(MESH_TG_IN_PROBE_BODY='' read_queue)"; [ "$out" = na ] || F "an empty probe body did not render na (got $out)"
  out="$(MESH_TG_IN_PROBE_BODY='{"ok":true,"result":{"pending_update_count":4}}' read_queue)"; [ "$out" = 4 ] || F "the queue count was not parsed out of a real getWebhookInfo body (got $out)"
  out="$(MESH_TG_IN_CRED=/nonexistent-tg-cred read_queue)"; [ "$out" = na ] || F "missing token did not render na (got $out)"
  # (11) last-in parses a REAL log shape, and refuses to invent an age for a junk/empty one.
  local tmp; tmp="$(mktemp)"
  printf '%s  VOICE  hi\n' "$(date -u -d '-90 min' +%FT%TZ)" > "$tmp"
  out="$(MESH_TG_IN_LOG="$tmp" read_lastin)"
  case "$out" in *\|89|*\|90|*\|91) : ;; *) F "a 90min-old inbound line did not age to ~90 (got $out)";; esac
  : > "$tmp"; [ "$(MESH_TG_IN_LOG="$tmp" read_lastin)" = "nodata|nodata" ] || F "an empty voice-in.log did not render nodata"
  printf 'no timestamp here\n' > "$tmp"
  [ "$(MESH_TG_IN_LOG="$tmp" read_lastin)" = "nodata|nodata" ] || F "an unparseable voice-in.log did not render nodata"
  rm -f "$tmp"
  # (12) --check MUST NOT write. The sense that writes its own liveness artifact forges it.
  local ts="$(mktemp)" tl="$(mktemp)" tm="$(mktemp)"; rm -f "$ts" "$tl" "$tm"
  MESH_TG_IN_STATE="$ts" MESH_TG_IN_LOGFILE="$tl" MESH_TG_IN_MARK="$tm" MESH_TG_IN_CHAT=0 \
    MESH_TG_IN_UNIT_CMD='echo "loaded|active|running|1"' MESH_TG_IN_JOURNAL_CMD='true' \
    MESH_TG_IN_PROBE_CMD='echo 0' MESH_TG_IN_LOG=/nonexistent-voice-in \
    bash "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" --check >/dev/null 2>&1
  { [ -e "$ts" ] || [ -e "$tl" ]; } && F "--check wrote the state/log artifact a watchdog reads for liveness"
  # ...and a real pass DOES write, with the verdict in it (a sandboxed state, never ~/.mesh).
  MESH_TG_IN_STATE="$ts" MESH_TG_IN_LOGFILE="$tl" MESH_TG_IN_MARK="$tm" MESH_TG_IN_CHAT=0 \
    MESH_TG_IN_UNIT_CMD='echo "loaded|active|running|1"' \
    MESH_TG_IN_JOURNAL_CMD="printf 'a %s\nb %s\nc %s\n' '$CONFLICT_RE' '$CONFLICT_RE' '$CONFLICT_RE'" \
    MESH_TG_IN_PROBE_CMD='echo 0' MESH_TG_IN_LOG=/nonexistent-voice-in \
    bash "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" >/dev/null 2>&1
  out="$(cat "$ts" 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$out" in *CONFLICT*) : ;; *) F "a real pass did not write the RED verdict to its state (got: ${out:-EMPTY})";; esac
  case "$out" in *"queue=0"*) : ;; *) F "the state line does not carry the queue leg";; esac
  rm -f "$ts" "$tl" "$tm"
  # (12b) THE PROCESS PROBE MUST NOT COUNT ITS OWN READER. This is the gate for the bug that made the
  #       first draft read HEARING with the unit stopped: the tool's own journalctl child carries
  #       "mesh-voice-rx.service" in its cmdline.
  out="$(printf 'journalctl journalctl --user -u mesh-voice-rx.service --since -15min\nbash /bin/bash -c mesh-tg-inbound --check\ngrep grep -c mesh-voice-rx\n' | _count_poller)"
  [ "$out" = 0 ] || F "the poller probe counted its own reader's processes (got $out, want 0)"
  out="$(printf 'python3 python3 /home/x/.local/bin/mesh-voice-rx\njournalctl journalctl -u mesh-voice-rx.service\n' | _count_poller)"
  [ "$out" = 1 ] || F "the poller probe missed a real python3 mesh-voice-rx (got $out, want 1)"
  # ...and end to end: unit failed + only decoy processes MUST be NOEAR, the reading that was green.
  out="$(decide loaded failed failed 0 44 0 0)"
  case "$out" in NOEAR\|1\|*) : ;; *) F "unit failed with no poller did not go RED NOEAR (got: ${out%%|*})";; esac

  # (13) REAL READ — every leg must produce a parseable value from THIS node's actual kernel/systemd/
  #      filesystem, not just from fixtures. A tool whose test only drives stubs has never touched the
  #      thing it wraps.
  out="$(read_unit)"
  case "$out" in *\|*\|*\|*) : ;; *) F "real unit read did not return 4 fields (got: $out)";; esac
  out="$(read_lastin)"
  case "$out" in nodata\|nodata|*Z\|[0-9]*) : ;; *) F "real voice-in.log read returned neither a timestamp nor nodata (got: $out)";; esac
  out="$(read_conflict)"
  case "$out" in na\|na|[0-9]*\|[0-9]*) : ;; *) F "real journal read returned neither a streak nor na (got: $out)";; esac
  out="$(read_queue)"
  case "$out" in na|[0-9]*) : ;; *) F "real queue probe returned neither a count nor na (got: $out)";; esac
  # (14) THE SCHEDULER'S ENVIRONMENT IS PART OF THE READ. Gate (13) above accepts `na|na`, and it had
  #      to — na is a legitimate value. That is exactly what made it blind to the one way na becomes
  #      PERMANENT: cron gives a job no XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS, so every scheduled
  #      tick degraded legs 1 and 3 to absent/na while an interactive run of the same code was green.
  #      Assert the read SURVIVES a scrubbed environment, by re-entering this script with the bus
  #      variables stripped — the same shape cron applies. Where there is no user bus or the unit is
  #      not here, there is no claim to make: skip, and SAY the skip, so an absent assertion is never
  #      read as a passing one.
  if [ -S "/run/user/$(id -u)/bus" ] && [ "$(systemctl --user show "$UNIT" -p LoadState --value 2>/dev/null)" = loaded ]; then
    out="$(env -u XDG_RUNTIME_DIR -u DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS "$0" --probe-env 2>/dev/null)"
    case "$out" in
      loaded\|*) : ;;
      *) F "under a cron-like env (no XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/DBUS) the unit leg reads '${out%%|*}' — legs 1+3 are blind on every scheduled tick" ;;
    esac
    case "$out" in
      *\|na\|na) F "under a cron-like env the conflict leg renders na — the rival-poller detector is dead under its own cadence" ;;
    esac
  else
    echo "  (14) skipped: no user bus, or $UNIT is not loaded on this node — nothing to assert" >&2
  fi

  [ "$fails" = 0 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAILED ($fails)" >&2; exit 1; }
  echo "smoke-test: ok (CONFLICT/UNPROVEN/BACKLOG/NOEAR/NODATA/QUIET/HEARING each driven RED-or-green; 'we cannot hear' != 'operator was silent'; streak is consecutive+reset; blind journal/probe render na not 0; --check writes nothing; the HOST GATE is spent only on systemd's own not-found and never on our own blindness, NOHOST reaches no board post while the control arm proves the post path was live, and the NOEAR sentence is node-local; all four legs really read on this node)"
  exit 0
}

case "${1:-}" in
  --test)   run_test ;;
  --check)  pass 0; exit $? ;;
  # test support for gate (14): print the two bus-dependent legs as this process actually sees them,
  # so the gate can re-enter under a scrubbed environment instead of asserting about one.
  --probe-env) printf '%s|%s\n' "$(systemctl --user show "$UNIT" -p LoadState --value 2>/dev/null)" "$(read_conflict)"; exit 0 ;;
  --status) [ -s "$STATE" ] && { cat "$STATE"; exit 0; }; echo "no reading yet ($STATE)"; exit 2 ;;
  ""|--run) pass 1; exit $? ;;
  *) echo "usage: mesh-tg-inbound [--check|--status|--test]" >&2; exit 64 ;;
esac
